Incidental Ectopic Beats (PACs/PVCs)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Asymptomatic ECG finding or mild skipped beat sensation | Normal structural echocardiogram | Absence of sustained arrhythmias

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-006
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of nocturnal chest thumping and perceived skipped beats requiring urgent assessment to exclude a malignant cardiac arrhythmia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate a new, distressing cardiac rhythm disturbance occurring at rest. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out atrial fibrillation, acute ischemic events, or hemodynamically unstable arrhythmias, preventing severe deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My heart suddenly skips a beat and then thumps hard in my chest as soon as I lie down to sleep.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden sensation of a skipped or missed heartbeat, accompanied by a brief flutter or thumping sensation in the chest. The symptom profile exhibits a distinct exacerbation during periods of rest or lying down at night. Despite the distressing sensation, the patient notes an absence of dyspnea, diaphoresis, or presyncope. The patient was completely asymptomatic prior to this evening.
Functional Status: Fully independent in all activities of daily living.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute anxiety regarding sudden cardiac death, disrupting sleep.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm (Irregular) | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Patient appears alert and anxious but is not in acute respiratory distress.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a generally regular rate but with intact peripheral pulses occasionally displaying a palpated pause. There is a lack of clinical signs of heart failure and a normal jugular venous pressure. Neurological examination incidentally reveals a subjective memory complaint and objective memory impairment on delayed recall testing, though there is preserved independent functioning globally.
Environmental Observations: A large collection of caffeinated energy drink cans is visible on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No proarrhythmic medications identified.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Incidental Ectopic Beats (PACs/PVCs) - Status: Stable, exacerbated by sympathomimetic intake.
Differentials:

Atrial Fibrillation - Ruled out due to the presence of a discernible, regular baseline rhythm interrupted only by isolated pauses, rather than an irregularly irregular rhythm.

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out due to the complete absence of chest pain, shortness of breath, and diaphoresis.

Ventricular Tachycardia - Ruled out due to preserved blood pressure, lack of presyncope, and immediate hemodynamic stability.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required urgent after-hours evaluation due to acute, severe distress from a perceived cardiac dysrhythmia occurring at rest. Telephone triage appropriately identified the risk of a hemodynamically compromising arrhythmia such as new-onset atrial fibrillation or frequent ventricular ectopy indicating ischemia. Objective physical examination confirmed normal vital signs, preserved blood pressure, and isolated ectopic beats without signs of heart failure. This direct clinical assessment firmly ruled out an acute cardiopulmonary emergency, halting an unnecessary ambulance dispatch while securing the patient’s safety until a formal outpatient Holter monitor can be applied.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided. The expected investigation results include an ambulatory Holter monitor demonstrating a low burden of premature atrial or ventricular contractions without structural heart disease, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via routine GP follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the frequency of skipped beats, replace all evening caffeinated beverages with herbal tea; decreasing systemic stimulant load will calm the cardiac conduction system and directly diminish ectopic firing.
Home-Based Services: None indicated at this time.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 7 days for a 24-hour Holter monitor referral and formal cognitive screening.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops sustained racing heart rates, chest pain, extreme dizziness, or loss of consciousness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending formal electrocardiographic monitoring; cardiac conditions can evolve.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves intermittent flares of ectopic beats heavily influenced by stress, fatigue, and stimulants, rarely leading to structural disease.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if the heart palpitations become constant, or if you feel faint, breathless, or develop pain in your chest or jaw.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Acute antiarrhythmic medications are withheld because benign extra beats do not require pharmacological suppression and the side effects outweigh the benefits.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate caffeine intake after midday and practice deep breathing exercises before sleep to lower sympathetic nervous system tone.

